import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "The server",
	description: "The rocx server platform: Roc compiled to native code in a Rust host. Every request on a thread of its own, streams as loops, crashes that end one call, and memory that comes back.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "layers", label: "What is Rust, what is Roc" },
		{ id: "calls", label: "A call, on a thread of its own" },
		{ id: "streams", label: "Streams" },
		{ id: "crashes", label: "A crash ends one call" },
		{ id: "memory", label: "Memory" },
		{ id: "islands", label: "Islands, rendered here" },
		{ id: "background", label: "Background loops, isolated calls" },
		{ id: "tcp", label: "Tcp and Random" },
		{ id: "running", label: "Running it" },
		{ id: "logging", label: "The log" },
		{ id: "deploying", label: "Deploying" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · the server"
				title="Roc compiled to native code, in a Rust host"
			>
				One executable: the app's Roc, as machine code, linked with a host written in Rust on tokio and
				hyper. The host answers HTTP, runs each call into Roc where it may wait, catches what crashes,
				and frees what it held. Roc decides everything a request comes to; the host carries it.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="layers">What is Rust, what is Roc</Heading>
			<p>
				The host gives Roc general things: HTTP, threads, Postgres connections, sockets, sleeping and
				waking, a crash that ends one call. What an app is built from is the platform's Roc, on top of
				those, and an app could write its own the same way:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Rust:</strong> HTTP (tokio, hyper), a pool of Postgres connections and one that listens,
					TCP and TLS sockets, live topics and rooms, background loops, isolated calls, actors' mailboxes
					and timers, a store in memory, secure random numbers, rendering islands.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Roc</strong> (<code>server/platform/*.roc</code>): migrations, the jobs queue and its
					workers, cron (its parser too), email (an SMTP client, MIME), actors' state and messages,
					<code>Db</code>'s decoding.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="calls">A call, on a thread of its own</Heading>
			<p>
				Each call into Roc (a request, a stream, a background loop) runs on a thread from tokio's
				blocking pool, with an 8 MiB stack, up to 1024 of them. So an effect may simply wait: a hosted
				function blocks on its future there, and holds up nothing else. Eight requests that each wait 0.3 s
				on Postgres take about 0.3 s together; the tests check it.
			</p>
			<p>
				A value Roc makes belongs to its call's thread until the call returns; after that the host owns it
				and moves it (a stream, to the thread that sends it), never sharing it. Calls nest: an isolated call
				(a job) runs inside the one that made it, with its own context; an actor's turn is a call of its
				own. Each call has a context: its transaction, the rooms it joined, the topics it listens to and its
				sockets, all given back when it ends.
			</p>

			<Heading id="streams">Streams</Heading>
			<p>
				An endpoint's events are a loop (<code>{"Events.stream(|{ send!, sleep! }| …)"}</code>), and the host
				runs it on a thread of its own for as long as it is open: <code>send!</code> writes an event to the
				response, <code>sleep!</code> waits there, and wakes early if the client goes, so its next
				<code>send!</code> answers <code>Err(Gone)</code> and <code>?</code> ends the loop. Fifty streams
				sleeping at once hold up no page. <a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>Events</a> shows one, and a
				component listening to it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="crashes">A crash ends one call</Heading>
			<p>
				Roc's ABI says <code>roc_crashed</code> must never return: it may end the process, or jump out of
				the Roc code that called it. The host does the second. A guard (<code>server/host/src/guard.c</code>)
				sets a jump target around every call into Roc; <code>roc_crashed</code> jumps back to it, and the
				call ends there:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>A request</strong> is answered 500, with the message under <code>--dev</code>.</li>
				<li><strong>A stream</strong> ends.</li>
				<li><strong>A job</strong> runs again later, as one that failed.</li>
				<li><strong>An actor's turn</strong> is logged and told to the asker, and the actor's state stays as it was.</li>
				<li><strong>A transaction</strong> the call had open is rolled back.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The frames a crash skips are Roc's and a small trampoline's, never the host's: no Rust destructor is
				skipped. An integer that overflows, or a <code>crash</code> in the app's code: either ends its call
				only.
			</p>

			<Heading id="memory">Memory</Heading>
			<p>
				A crash mustn't leak. While a call runs, each allocation it makes is linked into a list of its own.
				If the call returns, the answer's strings are copied out and the list freed (a stream keeps its list
				until the call that sends it); if it crashes, everything in its list is freed. Roc's reference counts
				are atomic in native builds, so a value moved to another thread stays right.
			</p>
			<p>
				The tests crash 200 requests at once, half of them after allocating 2,000 strings, and check that Roc
				holds no allocation afterwards (<code>--debug</code> serves the count at
				<code>/__rocx/allocations</code>). This site's tests render its pages' islands again and again and
				check the same.
			</p>

			<Heading id="islands">Islands, rendered here</Heading>
			<p>
				When a page the app answers has <code>{"<rocx-island data-island=\"name\">"}</code> elements, the host
				renders those islands into them (<code>server/host/src/signals.rs</code>), as the browser's runtime
				would: every component run once, every hole once, nothing tracked and nothing waiting, a memo
				computed when it is made, an endpoint a component reads answered by its route, in-process. The
				answers go in a script at the end of the page, each under its URL, so the browser reads them instead
				of asking. An island the page hasn't isn't run.
			</p>
			<p>
				A failure (a context nothing provides, a memo that failed) shows the nearest
				<code>{"<Errored>"}</code>'s fallback, as in the browser; a crash leaves the islands empty, and the
				browser renders them itself. A read (<code>Endpoint.get!</code>) is a GET of one of the app's own
				paths, which the app's <code>respond!</code> answers, behind the route's middleware. While the host
				renders, it is that request, made in-process (<code>Host.fetch!</code>):
				<code>accept: application/json</code>, the page's headers, cookies among them, the context the server
				started with, and the same answer the browser would get. Each URL is asked once a render, and its
				answer is noted whatever its status, unless it was read inside a <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code>, whose
				reads the browser never makes. An answer that isn't 200 fails the read, and the nearest
				<code>{"<Errored>"}</code> shows its status and text; a redirect makes the page that redirect. A
				server function (deprecated) is answered the same way, as <code>POST /rocx/call/…</code>
				(<code>Host.call!</code>). The site's tests compare what the host renders with what the browser's
				runtime renders, byte for byte, on every page with islands.
			</p>

			<Heading id="background">Background loops, isolated calls</Heading>
			<p>
				At start, the host asks the platform's Roc which loops to run in the background (the job workers,
				cron's ticker, a look at the SMTP server) and runs each on a thread of its own until the server
				stops. A loop sleeps with <code>Live.next!</code>, which wakes when what it listens to is published
				(Postgres's <code>NOTIFY</code> is published on <code>pg:channel</code>), when its time is up, or
				with <code>Gone</code> when the server stops. One that crashes is started again a second later.
			</p>
			<p>
				An isolated call runs Roc inside another call, guarded on its own: a crash in it ends it alone, and
				the caller is told, with why. A worker runs each job that way, so a job that crashes is a job that
				failed, and the worker goes on. An actor's turn is a guarded call of its own, which its mailbox in
				the host runs on a thread of the pool with the kind, key, state and message as strings, so a crash
				frees all of them. No lock is taken anywhere: one task routes every message to its actor's mailbox,
				and each actor handles its own, one at a time.
			</p>

			<Heading id="tcp">Tcp and Random</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Tcp.connect!</code> (or <code>connect_tls!</code>, or <code>start_tls!</code> on an open one)
				opens a socket for the call; <code>write_bytes!</code>, <code>read_exactly!</code>,
				<code>read_up_to!</code> and <code>read_until!</code> use it, each waiting on the call's thread. TLS is
				rustls, checked against the web's certificate authorities. A socket the call leaves open is closed
				when it ends. <code>Random.seed_u64!</code> is the operating system's randomness. The platform's SMTP
				client is written on these, and so is <a href={"${Paths.platform_postgres}#roc-pg"}>roc-pg</a>, a
				Postgres client in Roc.
			</p>

			<Heading id="running">Running it</Heading>
			<Code
				title="The app's executable"
				source={"<app> --port 8080 --host 127.0.0.1        where to listen (PORT)\n      --static DIR, --file /PATH=FILE     files served as they are\n      --context-file FILE                 what respond! is given besides each request\n      --database-url URL                  Postgres (DATABASE_URL): Db, migrations, jobs, cron, actors\n      --job-workers 4 --job-retry-ms 1000 jobs run at once; the first retry's wait, doubled after\n      --actor-idle-ms 900000              an actor idle this long is let go (0: never; kept in Postgres)\n      --smtp-url URL --mail-from ADDRESS  (SMTP_URL, MAIL_FROM); none: an outbox\n      --storage-url URL                   S3 (STORAGE_URL): Storage\n      --dev                               reload pages on restart, show crashes, GET /__rocx/mail\n      --debug                             GET /__rocx/allocations and /__rocx/mail"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain={Bool.True}
			/>
			<p>
				A request's body may be 16 MiB at most (413 beyond). Starting, the server takes its port first: one
				in use stops it there, before the database, the migrations or the background loops start, so
				nothing is left running. Stopping (SIGTERM, or ^C), the server stops
				listening and taking jobs, and waits up to ten seconds for the requests, streams and jobs still
				running. Without a database, what needs one says so when it is used; the rest works.
			</p>

			<Heading id="logging">The log</Heading>
			<p>
				Every line the server writes is an event: a level, a message and fields. The host's own lines
				(starting, stopping, a crash with its method, target and error, forwarding) and the app's go through
				one formatter, in the host. An app logs with <code>Log.info!</code>, and <code>debug!</code>,
				<code>warn!</code> and <code>error!</code>, its fields a record written as JSON;
				<code>Log.line!</code> is a message alone. The format is the app's to choose, in its program:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="An event, and the log's format"
				source={"Log.info!(\"gate failed\", { path: request.path, error: why })\n\nprogram = {\n\t..App.program({ context, middleware, crons }),\n\tlogs: { format: Auto }, # or Pretty, or Json\n}"}
			/>
			<Code
				title="The line, pretty and as JSON"
				source={"14:03:12 INFO  gate failed error=\"no session\" path=/admin\n{\"time\":\"2026-11-21T13:03:12.345Z\",\"level\":\"info\",\"msg\":\"gate failed\",\"error\":\"no session\",\"path\":\"/admin\"}"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain={Bool.True}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>Auto</code>, which <code>App.program</code> says, is <code>ROCX_LOG</code>
				(<code>pretty</code> or <code>json</code>) if it is set, or else pretty when stderr is a terminal,
				the level in colour, and JSON when it isn't: a server whose log is collected. A field can't take
				the place of <code>time</code>, <code>level</code> or <code>msg</code> (it is written
				<code>fields.msg</code>). Logging doesn't wait for stderr: the event goes on a queue, and a thread of
				its own writes the lines in order, flushing when the queue is empty, and before the process exits. A
				full queue (64k events) makes the caller wait rather than lose a line.
			</p>

			<Heading id="deploying">Deploying</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>node server/build.mjs app.roc out/app --target=arm64musl</code> (or <code>x64musl</code>)
				cross-compiles a static Linux executable from macOS: Rust's own musl target supplies the C library and
				the unwinder, and Zig compiles the host's C. Its migrations are in it; copy it into a container with
				the app's static files, give it a <code>DATABASE_URL</code>, and put a proxy in front for TLS. The platform's test
				app, built that way, runs every one of its tests in a container against <code>just db</code>'s
				Postgres.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>One server.</strong> Live rooms, topics and actors' mailboxes are in its memory (jobs and
					actors' state are in Postgres already).
				</li>
				<li><strong>HTTP/1.1, no TLS:</strong> put it behind a proxy.</li>
				<li><strong>A stack overflow in Roc</strong> still ends the process.</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="note" title="Built on">
				<p>
					tokio and hyper (HTTP), tokio-postgres with deadpool, rustls with ring (TLS, and random numbers),
					and Roc's glue for the types the header names (<code>just glue</code> writes
					<code>server/host/src/roc_platform_abi.rs</code>).
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
